1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:12,760
In one of our other videos, we showed you how to create a scan using some of BurpSuite

2
00:00:12,760 --> 00:00:16,040
Enterprise Edition's built-in scan configurations.

3
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:19,760
In this video, we'll look at the options you have for creating and using your own custom

4
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:22,480
scan configurations.

5
00:00:22,480 --> 00:00:27,120
From the settings menu, you can select scan configurations to see your entire library of

6
00:00:27,120 --> 00:00:32,520
both built-in configurations and any custom ones that you've created already.

7
00:00:32,520 --> 00:00:36,580
To create a new one, just click New Configuration.

8
00:00:36,580 --> 00:00:40,400
As you can see, most of the configuration options are grouped based on the phase of the scan

9
00:00:40,400 --> 00:00:43,920
that they relate to and what kind of behaviour they control.

10
00:00:43,920 --> 00:00:47,840
These are the same options that you might be familiar with from BurpSuite Professional.

11
00:00:47,840 --> 00:00:52,660
To begin with, all of the settings are the same as BurpScanner's standard configuration.

12
00:00:52,660 --> 00:00:56,300
You can then expand each area that you're interested in and make whatever changes you

13
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:57,980
want to.

14
00:00:57,980 --> 00:01:02,800
For example, during the crawl phase, BurpScanner usually attempts to create a new user whenever

15
00:01:02,800 --> 00:01:05,540
it identifies a registration form.

16
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:09,200
You might actually not want it to do this, for argument's sake, because you're already

17
00:01:09,200 --> 00:01:14,180
providing valid sets of credentials as application logins when you create the site.

18
00:01:14,180 --> 00:01:19,060
And you might not want these auto-generated BurpScanner users lingering on your website after the scan's

19
00:01:19,060 --> 00:01:20,120
finished.

20
00:01:20,120 --> 00:01:25,100
In this case, you could just go to the login function section and disable this feature.

21
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:30,180
You'll also want to give the configuration a sensible name so that you or other colleagues

22
00:01:30,180 --> 00:01:36,260
can identify it easily when creating scans.

23
00:01:36,260 --> 00:01:40,560
As you can apply multiple configurations to a scan, it generally makes more sense to create

24
00:01:40,560 --> 00:01:44,820
smaller configurations like this that only are just one or two settings.

25
00:01:44,820 --> 00:01:48,540
You can then combine them in all kinds of different ways when creating scans to set a

26
00:01:48,540 --> 00:01:51,780
whole range of more comprehensive overall configurations.

27
00:01:51,780 --> 00:01:56,660
Working in this way makes your individual configurations more reusable, but it's obviously completely

28
00:01:56,660 --> 00:01:57,660
up to you.

29
00:01:57,660 --> 00:02:01,200
If there's a specific combination of settings that you use a lot, then you can obviously

30
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:07,480
just change all of the settings you need in a single configuration.

31
00:02:07,480 --> 00:02:11,860
In addition to the settings that are specific to either the crawl or audit phase of the scan,

32
00:02:11,860 --> 00:02:15,660
there are a couple of extra sections down at the bottom here.

33
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:19,700
Under connections, you have various options for enabling the scan to authenticate with the

34
00:02:19,700 --> 00:02:21,420
destination web server.

35
00:02:21,420 --> 00:02:25,540
You can also upload any client TLS certificates that might be required.

36
00:02:25,540 --> 00:02:30,000
And finally, you can configure a connection to route the scan's traffic through a proxy

37
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:33,700
server if necessary.

38
00:02:33,700 --> 00:02:37,620
Under request throttling, you can limit how many requests the scan's allowed to make at

39
00:02:37,620 --> 00:02:42,660
the same time, and you can also enforce a slight delay between sending each request.

40
00:02:42,660 --> 00:02:46,520
This can be useful to avoid overloading your system resources when running lots of scans

41
00:02:46,520 --> 00:02:52,100
at the same time, and it can also help you avoid placing too much load on the target server.

42
00:02:52,100 --> 00:02:56,100
Once you're done making all the changes you want, just click save and the new configuration

43
00:02:56,100 --> 00:02:58,940
will be added to your library.

44
00:02:58,940 --> 00:03:02,820
If you now open the configuration, you can see that all of the areas that you haven't

45
00:03:02,820 --> 00:03:03,980
changed are collapsed.

46
00:03:03,980 --> 00:03:09,160
But the login function section that we did change is expanded to show that this configuration

47
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:13,620
will override one or more of the settings in this area.

48
00:03:13,620 --> 00:03:17,540
Back on the scan configurations page, you can delete custom configurations that you don't

49
00:03:17,540 --> 00:03:19,500
want anymore.

50
00:03:19,500 --> 00:03:24,580
And you can click this download icon here to export any of the scan configurations in JSON

51
00:03:24,580 --> 00:03:26,920
format.

52
00:03:26,920 --> 00:03:30,780
You can also import new configurations from a JSON file.

53
00:03:30,780 --> 00:03:35,420
This is the same format as BookSuite Professionals, so you can easily share your favourite configurations

54
00:03:35,420 --> 00:03:41,260
between both products if you want.

55
00:03:41,260 --> 00:03:45,460
If you now go to creator scan, you can see that your new configuration is available for

56
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:51,840
selection along with all the built-in ones.

57
00:03:51,840 --> 00:03:56,260
Notice that you also have the option to upload any configurations that you previously exported

58
00:03:56,260 --> 00:03:58,660
as a JSON file.

59
00:03:58,660 --> 00:04:03,260
So that's how to create and work with custom scan configurations in BurpSuite Enterprise Edition.

60
00:04:03,260 --> 00:04:04,260
So that's how to create and work with custom scan.

